WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.540
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, Episode 86.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:09.740
It's May 31st, 2026, and we're looking back across the past week to see which missions

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:14.760
advanced, which data sets landed, and what those steps actually mean for the work ahead.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:20.840
SpaceX's redesigned Starship V3 reached space on its first flight last week,

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:24.240
and the engineering teams are now deep in the post-flight review.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:29.580
That review will shape the return to flight attempt, which is meant to show clearer gains

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:33.540
in engine performance and in how the booster is brought back under control.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:37.880
Regulators still have to sign off before any new data is locked in,

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:43.140
yet hardware is already being stacked at the Texas site so the next test can move forward

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:48.800
without delay. What matters here is that each completed flight narrows the remaining unknowns

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:55.200
around orbital refueling and how quickly a booster can be ready again. From that launch cadence,

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.720
we turn to the sky itself. NASA's Transiting Exile Planet Survey Satellite has finished

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:06.520
an all-sky catalog that lists thousands of planets around stars close enough for follow-up work.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.980
The map shows how planet occurrence changes with stellar type and with location inside the galaxy,

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.780
and the transit signals were checked against background stars to keep false positives low.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:23.340
Astronomers now have already lists they can hand to larger telescopes for atmosphere studies,

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:28.860
and the next step is to line those targets up with ground-based radial velocity measurements so

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:34.680
masses and orbits can be pinned down more tightly. Shifting from distant worlds to our own,

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:40.500
Maven's latest passes over Mars picked up plasma behavior that doesn't match earlier models.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.460
The data show pockets where the solar wind presses in differently,

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:49.040
and that detail helps trace how the planet lost most of its original atmosphere.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.800
More orbiter passes are planned, so the same regions can be sampled again,

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:58.280
and the full set of measurements will feed into papers once processing finishes.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:05.380
Commercial work offers a different angle. Orbit fab and Thales Alina are studying how a docking

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:09.920
port can link with electric propulsion systems that run on xenon or similar gases.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.600
The test look at mechanical fit, fluid transfer, and electrical interfaces so future servicing

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:21.960
missions can top up propellant without bringing the satellite home. Results from this effort will

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.140
set the practical limits for what kinds of spacecraft can be refueled in orbit.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:32.340
The UK's new monitoring system adds another layer of protection. It pulls tracking data together

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.280
with defensive steps that cover both collision risk and radio interference.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:42.440
Fresh images release this week show the network now reaches farther across the orbital environment,

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:48.260
giving operators earlier notice when something drifts too close or starts to jam signals.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:53.780
Back on the ground, a plasma-based cleaning method is being tested for fabrics that would

00:02:53.780 --> 00:03:00.300
otherwise need water that simply won't exist on long surface missions. Lab runs show the ionized

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:05.840
gas breaks down organic bits at temperatures low enough for spacecraft use, which could stretch

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:11.140
the life of the few sets of clothing crews can carry. Next work will check whether repeated

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:18.040
exposures weaken the materials themselves. Meanwhile, the Shenzhu-22 return capsule brought back

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:24.400
another round of station samples covering materials, fluids, and biology. Ground labs are starting

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.320
the detailed analysis, and this flight marks the 10th recovery supporting Tian Gong research,

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:34.300
with more returns already scheduled through the rest of the year. From returned hardware,

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:40.180
we move to planet formation still in progress. Researchers at the University of Warwick are using

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:44.980
all my images to weigh young planets by the gaps they carve in their dusty disks.

00:03:45.860 --> 00:03:51.780
Gap width and depth line up with planet mass in the models, letting astronomers study systems that

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:58.480
are still embedded in gas and therefore only a few million years old. The method sidesteps the need

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:05.660
to spot the planets directly, which opens a much earlier window on growth. ESA's smile spacecraft

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:11.840
has settled into its long elliptical orbit after launch. From that path, the instruments will watch

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:16.940
the solar wind meeting Earth's magnetosphere, swinging repeatedly between the dayside boundary

00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:22.860
and the stretched out tail, so both regions are covered in a single orbit. Farther afield,

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:29.260
construction has begun on a new facility in western China that will collect spectra from thousands

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:35.600
of galaxies at once, giving large-scale surveys a faster way to map how galaxies move and evolve

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:43.240
closer to home, May closes with a second full moon rising on the 30th. The geometry is tight,

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:49.400
so the moment the moon clears the horizon is brief and depends on local skyline and exact timing.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:55.220
Photographers planning to catch the low, large appearance are already checking sites because

00:04:55.220 --> 00:05:01.020
atmospheric conditions can change how the disk looks from one minute to the next. A few days earlier

00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:08.140
on the 25th, Mercury, Venus, and Mars lined up low in the evening sky. The grouping was short lived,

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:13.420
giving observers a narrow chance to see the three planets together before orbital motion pulled them

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:19.220
apart again. That wraps the week's developments. The next starship flight will show whether booster

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:26.100
recovery has improved, TESS targets will start moving into atmosphere studies, and Maven's plasma

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:30.940
measurements will keep feeding atmospheric loss models. I'm Patrick and Vancouver,

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:36.720
thanks for listening, and I'll be back tomorrow with whatever comes next. This episode used AI

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:41.400
voice synthesis of my voice. Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
